# Prettier's ignore scope for the whole workspace. The `check` script points here with
# --ignore-path, so build output and generated code are excluded once, at the root, instead of
# once per app. Patterns are gitignore syntax; the ones without a slash match at any depth, so
# they hold whichever directory prettier runs from.

# Dependencies and package-manager state
node_modules/
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Build output — `build` rewrites it, so any formatting applied here is undone by the next run
out/
dist/
.output/
.vite/
.tanstack/
.turbo/
coverage/

# Generated code — owned by its generator, which rewrites it wholesale
routeTree.gen.ts
plugins/*/scripts/lib/

# Prose that prettier does not own — documentation, task files and SKILL.md bodies: hand-formatted,
# read as much in a GitHub blob and a Claude context window as in an editor, and reflowing them
# would rewrite line breaks that were chosen.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy: `/plugins/` is the published plugin
# tree, and only the one at the repo root is that. A bare `plugins/` would match at any depth.
/plugins/

# Concept-skill markdown is NOT in the enforced set, at any depth. This is a decision, not an
# artifact of where the pattern happens to anchor.
#
# The anchoring above used to decide it by accident, and decided it inconsistently: `check` runs
# per workspace, and only apps/maestro defines one, so the 9 concept skills under
# apps/maestro/.claude/skills/ were checked while the 5 under the repo root's own .claude/skills/
# were not — same kind of file, same authors, opposite treatment, for no reason anyone chose.
#
# Prettier is the wrong tool for these bodies specifically. It pads markdown tables to aligned
# columns, and it measures those columns with a width count that disagrees with itself on the em
# dashes this prose style uses everywhere — so it emits tables whose separator row is a different
# width from its content rows, then reports them as correctly formatted. What it produces is not
# more consistent than what was written by hand; it is differently inconsistent, and it churns on
# every edit. These files are read in GitHub blobs and Claude context windows, where the chosen
# line breaks are the formatting.
#
# Markdown only. Scripts that ship alongside a skill (`scripts/*.mjs`) are code and stay checked.
**/.claude/skills/**/*.md
